<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nogais</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nogai people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nogais are a Turkic people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> and the steppes north of the Caspian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Seas</a>, traditionally named after Nogai, a 13th-century commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. From the 15th century they formed the Nogai Horde, a nomadic confederation between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Ural, and later groups lived under the Crimean Khanate. Most now live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavropol">Stavropol</a> Krai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachay-cherkessia">Karachay-Cherkessia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; they are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nogais.jpg" alt="Nogais" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Polypone · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_r%C3%A9partition_noga%C3%AFs.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
